[Rails] Easily enable Yandex.Metrika support in your Rails application.
&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;
By default this gem will output Yandex.Metrika code for every page automagically, if it's configured correctly. This is done by adding: &lt;br/&gt;
Yandex::Metrika.counter_id = '123456' &lt;br/&gt;
to your `config/environment.rb`, inserting your own COUNTER_ID. This can be discovered by looking at the value of "new Ya.Metrika(123456)" in the Javascript code.

Requires a hash with three key values   
       @workspace = {
      'qualtrics_id' =&gt; ENV['qualtrics_id'],
      'qualtrics_library' =&gt; ENV['qualtrics_library_id'],
      'study_workspace_name' =&gt; 'Shuti-Example'
    }

Unlike ordinary set(see Ruby documentation for "set" library), multiset can contain two or more same items.

 Set[:a,:b,:c,:b,:b,:c] # =&gt; #&lt;Set: {:b, :c, :a}&gt;
 Multiset[:a,:b,:c,:b,:b,:c] # =&gt; #&lt;Multiset:#3 :b, #2 :c, #1 :a&gt;

Multisets are typically used for counting elements and their appearances in collections.

Unlike ordinary set(see Ruby documentation for "set" library), multiset can contain two or more same items.

 Set[:a,:b,:c,:b,:b,:c] # =&gt; #&lt;Set: {:b, :c, :a}&gt;
 Multiset[:a,:b,:c,:b,:b,:c] # =&gt; #&lt;Multiset:#3 :b, #2 :c, #1 :a&gt;

Multisets are typically used for counting elements and their appearances in collections.

